insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-data-driven-forecast-is', 1, 'EKENE-P1 carries 48 months of rates. Which index does the engine give its last month?', '["Month 48, since the engine numbers months from 1, as a calendar count of months does.", "Index 0, since the engine stores the newest month first and counts backward.", "Month 47, because the engine counts from 0 and month 0 is the first month on production.", "Month 12 of year 4, since a monthly series is indexed by year and by month."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine counts months from 0: month 0 is the first month on production, so a 48-month series ends at month 47. Numbering from 1 would put the last month at 48, which is the month step 1 of a forecast stands for. The series runs oldest month first, and the engine indexes months with a single whole number, never by year and month.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-data-driven-forecast-is', 2, 'Which inputs does an exponential smoothing method in this course read when it forecasts a well?', '["Only the rate series, one monthly average rate per month, oldest first.", "The rate series together with the choke setting and the flowing pressure of every month it covers.", "A decline curve fitted first, whose shape the smoothing follows.", "Each month''s oil rate divided by its water cut, so that the method smooths the rate of total liquid."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A forecasting method here reads only the series itself: no pressure, no choke setting, no reservoir model. The engine runs no regression of rate on other variables such as choke or pressure, fits no decline curve of its own before smoothing, and reads no water cut. Each input the distractors add is something the method cannot see.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-data-driven-forecast-is', 3, 'Which of these does the forecasting engine return?', '["The EUR of the well, found by summing the forecast rates down to an economic limit that the caller states.", "Cumulative production to date, returned beside the fitted values of every month in the series.", "A seasonal Holt-Winters forecast whenever the series spans at least two full years of months.", "A rate at each future step from one fitted smoothing method."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine forecasts a rate at each future step. It computes no reserves, no EUR and no cumulative production; those belong to the decline curve analysis course. It builds no seasonal smoothing at all, so a Holt-Winters forecast never comes back whatever the length of the series.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-data-driven-forecast-is', 4, 'When this course uses the words machine learning, how is the method named?', '["As a neural network, the one learning method this engine builds and trains on the Ekene wells.", "By what it is: here exponential smoothing, with its free parameters fitted by least squares.", "As a regression of rate on choke and on pressure, fitted to each producing well in its turn.", "Loosely, as any automated judgement, since the name of the method inside does not matter."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course names a method by what it is: exponential smoothing, with free parameters fitted by the least one-step sum of squared errors. The engine builds no neural network and runs no regression of rate on choke or pressure; regression belongs to the machine learning course. Calling any automated judgement machine learning is the loose meaning the course legislates against.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-data-driven-forecast-is', 5, 'What does the course state about where the Ekene production rates came from?', '["They are the monthly allocations of a real field, anonymised by renaming the wells.", "They are synthetic, drawn by one generator on the stated seed 20260925 and rounded to 0.1 bbl/d.", "They are real well tests cleaned by the data quality course before any lesson was allowed to use them.", "A fresh draw is made at each session, so every learner meets different rates."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Ekene field is synthetic: one generator draws every series through the platform''s seeded random numbers on the seed 20260925 and rounds each rate to the tenth of a bbl/d. The same inputs give the same series anywhere, so no session draws new rates. No real field and no real well test sits behind the series.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-data-driven-forecast-is', 6, 'Which Ekene well carries a facility-limited plateau, and what does the plateau hold?', '["EKENE-P2, which holds 1500.000000 bbl/d over its three months before the workover lifts the rate.", "EKENE-P4, whose noisy allocation averages out at 1500.000000 bbl/d across its first year.", "EKENE-P1, which holds its first rate of 1176.100000 for nine months before its decline begins.", "EKENE-P3, held at exactly 1500.000000 bbl/d for months 0 to 8."]'::jsonb, 3, 'EKENE-P3 holds exactly 1500.000000 bbl/d for months 0 to 8, counted from 0, and declines after. EKENE-P2 carries the shut-in and the workover, EKENE-P4 the noisy allocation starting at 815.700000, and EKENE-P1 declines from its very first month, 1176.100000 then 1153.400000.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-data-driven-forecast-is', 7, 'EKENE-P2 reads 0.000000 in months 22 to 24, counted from 0. What are those three values?', '["A shut-in: real rates of 0, which a smoothing method reads like any other month.", "Missing months written as 0, which the engine fills by averaging the months on either side of them.", "Values the engine refuses by name, since 0 is not a rate it accepts.", "Months the engine drops before any fit, so that the smoothing sees the series with the gap closed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Months 22 to 24 of EKENE-P2 are a planted shut-in at rate 0. A rate of 0 is a real, finite value, so the engine neither refuses it nor drops it, and a smoothing method reads it as a rate like any other. The engine fills no month at all; a missing month would be refused by name.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-data-driven-forecast-is', 8, 'EKENE-P6, the new well, has three months. What does fitting holt on it return?', '["A refusal naming `y`: holt needs four months.", "A fit scored on all three months, since the start of holt takes nothing from the series itself.", "A fit with 1 scored error, the least the method can work with.", "Two scored errors, months 1 and 2."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Holt spends months 0 and 1 on the start, the level and the trend, so on three months it scores only month 2: 1 scored error. Three months is exactly the fewest holt accepts, so the fit is not refused. The start is taken from the series, which is why months 0 and 1 are not scored.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-data-driven-forecast-is', 9, 'In this course, what is a fitted value?', '["Any h-step forecast made past the last month, which the engine returns in its `forecast` list.", "The level l_t, computed once month t''s rate has arrived.", "The rate a straight line fitted through every month of the series gives at month t.", "A one-step forecast, made at month t - 1 for month t inside the series."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course calls a one-step forecast a fitted value: f_t is made at month t - 1 for month t, and the engine returns them as `fitted`. The h-step forecasts past the last month are the `forecast` list. The level l_t is made after month t arrives, so it is a state of the method. No straight line through the months is fitted by this engine.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-data-driven-forecast-is', 10, 'A 48-month series is fitted with h 12. Which month does index 0 of the `forecast` list stand for?', '["Month 48, one step past the last month, month 47.", "The last month on production, month 47, forecast a second time from the final state of the fit.", "Index 0 means month 0, since every list the engine returns begins at the first month.", "Month 24, since the forecast list is laid over the second half of a 48-month series."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Index 0 of `forecast` is one step past the last month. The last month of a 48-month series is month 47, so step 1 forecasts month 48. The `fitted` list starts at month 0; the `forecast` list starts past the end, and no h-step forecast is made for a month inside the series.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-data-driven-forecast-is', 11, 'A fit is run with `h` left out. What does the engine return?', '["A refusal naming `h`, because a forecast needs at least one step past the last month to return.", "Twelve h-step forecasts, the default the engine sets for any series of monthly rates.", "Every fitted value and residual, with an empty `forecast`, since h is 0 when left out.", "Ten thousand forecast steps, the largest h the engine accepts, as a default."]'::jsonb, 2, 'When `h` is left out it is 0, so a fit alone returns an empty `forecast` and still returns every fitted value and residual. A value of 0 is accepted, so there is no refusal. The engine sets no default of twelve; 10000 is the largest h accepted, never a default.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-data-driven-forecast-is', 12, 'Simple smoothing at alpha 0.3 on EKENE-P1 gives month 2 a residual of -90.690000. What does the sign tell you?', '["It forecast low: the fitted value, 1169.290000, sat below month 2''s rate of 1078.600000.", "The fitted value, 1169.290000, sat above the rate of 1078.600000.", "The level fell by 90.690000 bbl/d over the month.", "A fall of 90.690000 bbl/d in the rate itself over the month, read from the series."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A residual is the rate less the fitted value: 1078.600000 less 1169.290000 is -90.690000, so a negative residual means the forecast was high. The level moved from 1169.290000 to 1142.083000, a smaller fall. The rate itself fell from 1153.400000 to 1078.600000, which is a different difference from the residual.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-data-driven-forecast-is', 13, 'EKENE-P1 is passed with month 5 set to null. The engine replies, in its own words: "y[5] must be a finite number: fill or drop missing values first". What has it done?', '["Refused the whole call, naming index 5; filling or dropping the month is left to the caller.", "Filled month 5 with the mean of months 4 and 6, then fitted the method as usual.", "Dropped month 5 and fitted the other months, recording the gap in the basis block.", "Fitted months 0 to 4 and returned a warning for every month past the gap at month 5."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A null month stops the call: the engine returns `error` and `field` and no result, naming the first index it meets, counted from 0. It fills nothing, drops nothing and fits no part of the series. Whether to fill month 5 or drop it is the caller''s decision, and the data quality course teaches how to make it.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-data-driven-forecast-is', 14, 'A fit asks for the method ''arima''. Which field does the refusal name?', '["`y`, in the engine''s words \"y must be an array of numbers\", since a method must come first.", "No field at all: the engine falls back to ses and returns that fit with a warning beside it.", "`h`, since an ARIMA forecast needs a number of steps.", "`method`, in the engine''s words \"method must be ''ses'', ''holt'' or ''damped''\"."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine offers three methods and refuses any other by naming `method`, with a message that lists all three. It builds no ARIMA and falls back to no default method. The message about `y` is the refusal for a series that is not an array, and `h` has nothing to do with the method named.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-data-driven-forecast-is', 15, 'How does a refusal differ from a result in this engine?', '["A refusal is a full result with a caution attached, naming the input that caused it.", "It returns the fit on whichever months were valid, with the invalid ones listed by index.", "A refusal carries `error` and `field` and no result; any figure in it belongs to the message.", "It holds the last good result of an earlier call, flagged in the basis as out of date."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every function returns either a result object or an object with `error` and `field`, where `field` names the input it refused. A refusal carries no number of its own and no partial fit. A caution attached to a returned result is a warning, a different thing, which a fit carries only if its search ran out of effort.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-simple-exponential-smoothing', 1, 'In the engine''s simple exponential smoothing, what is the fitted value for month t?', '["The average alpha y_t + (1 - alpha) l_{t-1}, taken after month t arrives.", "The level from the month before, l_{t-1}.", "An equally weighted mean of every rate up to and including month t.", "Month t - 1''s rate, whatever value alpha has been set to."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The basis reads f_t = l_{t-1}: the fitted value is the level left by the month before. The weighted average alpha y_t + (1 - alpha) f_t is the new level, made once month t''s rate is in, so it cannot forecast month t. The weights on past months shrink by 1 - alpha each month back, so they are unequal, and the fitted value equals the month before''s rate only at alpha 1.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-simple-exponential-smoothing', 2, 'At alpha 0.3 on EKENE-P1, month 1 has rate 1153.400000 and fitted value 1176.100000. What level does month 1 end on?', '["1142.083000, the level the method reaches as soon as month 1''s rate arrives.", "It jumps to 1153.400000, since the level always takes the newest rate.", "1176.100000, held unchanged until month 2.", "1169.290000, which is 0.3 of the rate plus 0.7 of the fitted value."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The level update is alpha y_t + (1 - alpha) f_t: 0.3 x 1153.400000 + 0.7 x 1176.100000 is 1169.290000. The level 1142.083000 is month 2''s, one month later. The level takes the newest rate only at alpha 1, and it moves from month 1 on, since month 1''s rate is the first update.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-simple-exponential-smoothing', 3, 'What does alpha weight in simple exponential smoothing?', '["The newest month''s rate, with 1 - alpha going to the old forecast.", "The oldest month, so a large alpha anchors the level at month 0 for good.", "Each newest change in the level, set against the trend carried from before.", "The share of months the SSE scores."]'::jsonb, 0, 'alpha is the weight the newest month gets, and 1 - alpha goes to the forecast it replaces. A large alpha follows the newest rates; the level is anchored at month 0 only at alpha 0. Weighting the newest change in level against the old trend is beta''s job in the methods with a trend, and alpha has no say in which months are scored.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-simple-exponential-smoothing', 4, 'Where does the engine start simple smoothing''s level before month 1 is forecast?', '["From the mean of the first twelve months, worked out before the recursion begins.", "A starting level fitted by the search as one more free parameter beside alpha.", "At the first observation, l_1 = y_1, so month 1''s fitted value is month 0''s rate.", "Zero, so that the level climbs toward the rates across the first few months."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine anchors the level at the first observation, l_1 = y_1, and on EKENE-P1 month 1''s fitted value 1176.100000 is month 0''s rate. It averages no early months and fits no starting level; an `initialLevel` can be given, and then it is held fixed. A start at 0 is not the engine''s rule.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-simple-exponential-smoothing', 5, 'Simple smoothing is fitted on EKENE-P1''s 48 months. How many residuals does its SSE sum?', '["All 48, one per month, with month 0''s residual set to 0.", "47, from index 1 on, since month 0 has no fitted value.", "46, since the first two months are spent on the start.", "12, the months the h-step forecast is going to cover."]'::jsonb, 1, '`scoredFrom` is 1 for simple smoothing and the basis reads "errors scored from index 1 (0-based), 47 of them". Month 0 has no fitted value, so its residual is null and never scored. Spending two months on the start is the rule for holt and damped, which score 46, and the h-step forecasts are never part of the SSE.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-simple-exponential-smoothing', 6, 'At alpha 0.3 on EKENE-P1, an `initialLevel` of 1150.000000 is given. What changes?', '["Nothing in the numbers: the start is noted in the basis while the recursion still begins at y_1.", "The engine refuses it, since the level must start at month 0''s rate of 1176.100000.", "The last months move most, because the weight on the start grows as the recursion runs on.", "Month 1''s fitted value becomes 1150.000000 and the SSE moves from 312538.074209 to 297403.122315."]'::jsonb, 3, 'With an `initialLevel` given, the rule reads "l_1 = initialLevel", so the first fitted value is 1150.000000 and the SSE falls from 312538.074209 to 297403.122315. A number is accepted; only a value that is not a number is refused. At alpha 0.3 the start''s weight shrinks by 0.7 each month, so it matters most for the first months.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-simple-exponential-smoothing', 7, 'At alpha 0.3 on EKENE-P1, every one of the 12 h-step forecasts is 221.171043, while the last month is 211.400000. Why?', '["The forecast is the mean of the last twelve months, all of which lie above month 47.", "The engine holds a forecast at its final level so that it cannot fall below the series.", "Simple smoothing has no trend, so each step is the final level, which trails a falling well from above.", "Alpha 0.3 is too small to fit, so the engine freezes the forecast at the level it has."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis ends "forecast l_n at every step": with no trend, every h-step forecast is the final level. On a declining well the level trails the rates from above, so l_n, 221.171043, sits above the last rate. No mean of recent months is taken, the engine holds no forecast up by rule, and alpha 0.3 is a given value that runs as stated.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-simple-exponential-smoothing', 8, 'With alpha given and held fixed on EKENE-P1, how does the SSE move as alpha rises from 0 to 1?', '["It falls all the way, from 25246877.490000 at alpha 0 to 49562.030000 at alpha 1.", "It is lowest at alpha 0.5, 130321.602967, and rises again as alpha moves on toward 1.", "Upward throughout, since a larger alpha lets more noise into every level.", "It is the same at every alpha, since alpha changes only the h-step forecast."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The alpha table on EKENE-P1 shows the SSE falling at every row, from 25246877.490000 at alpha 0 through 130321.602967 at 0.5 to 49562.030000 at 1. On a steady decline, the more weight the newest month gets, the less the fitted values lag. alpha moves every level, so the fitted values and the SSE move with it.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-simple-exponential-smoothing', 9, 'What does simple smoothing do with alpha 0 given?', '["The naive forecast, with each fitted value being the month before.", "A refusal, since the engine accepts an alpha only above 0.", "It forecasts 211.400000, EKENE-P1''s last rate, at every step of the forecast list.", "The level never moves from y_1, so EKENE-P1 is forecast at 1176.100000 for ever."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At alpha 0 each new level is all old forecast and none of the new rate, so the level stays at y_1: month 0''s 1176.100000 is every fitted value and every h-step forecast, with the largest SSE in the table, 25246877.490000. The naive forecast is alpha 1. Both ends of 0 to 1 are accepted, and 211.400000 is the forecast at alpha 1.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-simple-exponential-smoothing', 10, 'At alpha 1 simple smoothing forecasts 211.400000 on EKENE-P1 at every step. What is that forecast called?', '["A damped trend''s limit, the rate a fading trend levels off at.", "The naive forecast: the last month''s rate, carried to every step.", "Its starting level, carried unchanged from month 0 to the end.", "The mean level, averaged over all 48 months of EKENE-P1."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At alpha 1 the level is always the newest rate, so each fitted value is the month before and the h-step forecast is the last month''s 211.400000: the naive forecast. A damped limit comes from a trend, and simple smoothing has none. The starting level 1176.100000 is what alpha 0 carries, and no mean of the months is taken.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-simple-exponential-smoothing', 11, 'With alpha left free, which 48-month Ekene well fits simple smoothing''s alpha inside the box?', '["EKENE-P4, the noisy allocation, at 0.528376.", "EKENE-P1, whose clean decline stops short of the bound at 0.528376.", "EKENE-P3, where the plateau holds the fitted alpha at 0.5.", "EKENE-P5, whose steep fall pulls the fitted alpha down to 0.1."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Fitted, alpha stops on its upper bound 1 on EKENE-P1, EKENE-P2, EKENE-P3 and EKENE-P5, and `atBounds` lists "alpha = 1" on each. Only the noisy allocation, EKENE-P4, fits alpha inside the box, at 0.528376, with `atBounds` none. The values 0.5 and 0.1 are rows of the alpha table where alpha was given.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-simple-exponential-smoothing', 12, 'Why does a steady decline push simple smoothing''s fitted alpha onto its bound 1?', '["The grid ends at 1, and the fit prefers the last grid point on a tie.", "Alpha 1 is what the engine returns when the search cannot find a minimum.", "Every older rate sits above the next one, so any weight on it adds lag.", "On a decline the fit weights the oldest month most, and 1 is that weight."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fitted value is a weighted average of past rates. On a well that falls every month, each past rate is above the next, so weight on older months pulls the fitted value up and away; the least lag comes from all the weight on the newest month. A grid tie keeps the earlier point, the search always stops at a minimum it can find, and alpha 1 weights the newest month.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-simple-exponential-smoothing', 13, 'An alpha of 1.2 is passed. What does the engine reply?', '["A fit at alpha 1, the nearest value inside the box, with a warning attached.", "Refused by naming `y`: \"y must be an array of numbers\".", "It fits at 1.2, since only a fitted alpha is kept inside 0 to 1.", "A refusal naming `alpha`: \"alpha must be a number from 0 to 1 (inclusive)\"."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A given alpha outside 0 to 1 is refused by name, in the engine''s own words "alpha must be a number from 0 to 1 (inclusive)"; -0.1 gets the same reply. A refusal returns no fit, so nothing is moved to the bound, and the field named is the one at fault, `alpha`; the message about `y` is for a series that is not an array. A weight outside 0 to 1 would not be an average, which is why a given alpha is held to the same box.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-simple-exponential-smoothing', 14, 'A `beta` is passed with the method ''ses''. What happens?', '["It is ignored, since ses has no trend, and the fit comes back as usual.", "Refused: \"beta applies to ''holt'' and ''damped'' only: ''ses'' has no trend\".", "Refused: \"phi applies to ''damped'' only: ''ses'' has no trend to damp\".", "Accepted, and the fit is switched to holt with that beta, noted in the basis."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Simple smoothing has no trend, so the engine refuses anything that would set one, naming `beta` with the message in the key. The message about phi is the refusal for a phi passed to ses, which names `phi`. The engine does not silently ignore a parameter or change the method the caller asked for.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-simple-exponential-smoothing', 15, 'Which values of `h` does the engine accept when fitting?', '["A whole number from 0 to 10000; -1, 1.5 and 10001 are each refused.", "Any whole number from 1 to 10000, since h 0 would return no forecast.", "Any number above 0, with a fraction such as 1.5 rounded up to 2 steps.", "Up to 100000, the most values a series may carry."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The refusal reads "h must be a whole number from 0 to 10000", and -1, 1.5 and 10001 each draw it. h 0 is accepted and returns an empty `forecast`. Nothing is rounded, and 100000 is the most values a series may carry, a different limit.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-holts-linear-trend', 1, 'Holt''s recursion forecasts month t from the state left at month t - 1. Which expression gives that fitted value?', '["l_{t-1} + b_{t-1}: last month''s level plus last month''s trend.", "l_{t-1} + phi b_{t-1}, the damped fitted value.", "The level l_{t-1} alone.", "beta times the level plus 1 - beta times the trend, both from month t - 1."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Holt''s basis reads f_t = l_{t-1} + b_{t-1}. Multiplying the trend by phi is the damped trend''s fitted value. Holt adds the trend inside the series as well as past it, and beta weights the change in level against the old trend inside the trend update, never the fitted value.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-holts-linear-trend', 2, 'What does beta weight in Holt''s trend update?', '["The newest rate against the old forecast, which is alpha''s role in simple smoothing.", "How strongly the trend is damped at each step once the series has ended.", "The newest change in level, l_t - l_{t-1}, against the old trend.", "Which share of the level the trend adds to each month''s fitted value."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The trend update is b_t = beta (l_t - l_{t-1}) + (1 - beta) b_{t-1}: beta weights the newest change in level and 1 - beta the old trend. Weighting the newest rate is alpha''s job in the level update. Damping is phi''s, in the damped method, and the whole trend is added to the level in the fitted value, whatever beta is.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-holts-linear-trend', 3, 'Holt''s state after month 1 of EKENE-P1 (alpha 0.5, beta 0.2 given) is a level of 1153.400000 and a trend of -22.700000. Which fitted value does month 2 receive?', '["1153.400000, since the level is carried without its trend until month 3.", "1104.650000, the level once month 2''s own rate has been taken in.", "1078.600000, month 2''s rate, forecast exactly by the way the start is built.", "1130.700000, the level plus the trend."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The fitted value is l_{t-1} + b_{t-1}: 1153.400000 plus -22.700000 is 1130.700000. The level 1104.650000 comes after month 2''s rate of 1078.600000 arrives, so it is no forecast of month 2. The month forecast exactly by construction is month 1, and the trend is added from the first forecast on.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-holts-linear-trend', 4, 'How does the engine start Holt''s linear trend?', '["The trend starts at 0 and builds from the first changes.", "l_1 = y_1 and b_1 = y_2 - y_1, so EKENE-P1 starts on a trend of -22.700000.", "Its starting trend is fitted by the search as a parameter alongside alpha and beta.", "From a least-squares line through the first twelve months."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine anchors the level at the first rate and takes the trend from the first two: on EKENE-P1 `initial.trend` is 1153.400000 less 1176.100000, -22.700000. It starts no trend at 0, fits no starting state, and draws no line through early months; a given `initialTrend` is the only other start.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-holts-linear-trend', 5, 'Why is month 1''s residual null in a Holt fit started by the engine?', '["f_2 = l_1 + b_1 lands on month 1''s rate by construction, so its residual of 0 says nothing.", "Month 1 counts as a missing month, which the engine fills and then leaves out of scoring.", "Scoring waits until the trend has settled, and the engine judges it settled from month 2.", "Month 1 is scored inside the SSE and only left out of the list the result returns."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The trend b_1 was built from month 1''s rate, so the level plus that trend lands exactly on it: a residual of 0 by construction, the answer used to set the question. The engine does not score it, `residuals` is null at index 1 and `scoredFrom` is 2. No month is missing, no settling test is run, and the SSE leaves month 1 out.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-holts-linear-trend', 6, 'On 48 months, how many errors do ses and holt each score?', '["48 each, one per month.", "46 for ses and 47 for holt, since a trend adds one scored month.", "47 each, since both spend only month 0 on their start.", "47 for ses, from index 1; 46 for holt, from index 2."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Simple smoothing spends only month 0, `scoredFrom` 1, 47 errors. Holt spends month 0 on the level and month 1 on the trend, `scoredFrom` 2, 46 errors. No method scores month 0, which has no fitted value, and the trend costs a scored month.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-holts-linear-trend', 7, 'Holt at alpha 0.5 and beta 0.2 on EKENE-P1 has SSE 29230.297102. What MSE does the engine report?', '["The SSE over all 48 months, month 0 and month 1 included in the count.", "29230.297102 itself, since the engine reports one figure for both.", "635.441241, the SSE divided by the 46 scored errors.", "The SSE over 47 errors, the divisor ses uses on the same months."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis reads "SSE / number of scored errors": 29230.297102 over 46 is 635.441241. Counting the unscored months would add nothing to the sum and inflate the divisor, flattering the MSE with months the method was handed. The SSE and MSE are separate fields, and ses''s 47 is a different count.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-holts-linear-trend', 8, 'Why does the course compare methods by MSE whenever methods are set side by side?', '["The MSE penalises every extra parameter, so a method with more of them must always score worse.", "ses scores 47 errors and holt and damped 46, so their SSEs sum different numbers of terms.", "The SSE takes in month 0, which no method in the engine is able to forecast.", "An MSE is in bbl/d, while an SSE carries no unit and so cannot be read at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'An SSE over 47 errors and one over 46 add up different numbers of terms, and the MSE divides each by its own count, putting them on one footing. The MSE adds no penalty for parameters. Month 0 is never scored by any method. An MSE is in squared bbl/d, and an SSE carries a unit too.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-holts-linear-trend', 9, 'Suppose the Holt start is supplied as `initialTrend` -25.000000 on EKENE-P1. How does scoring shift?', '["Month 1 is scored: `scoredFrom` becomes 1, 47 errors, and month 1 is forecast at 1151.100000.", "Nothing changes: the engine still spends month 1 on the start and scores from index 2.", "The level starts at -25.000000 in place of month 0''s rate of 1176.100000.", "Month 0 is scored as well, since the start no longer needs any rate at all."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With `initialTrend` given, the rule reads "l_1 = y_1, b_1 = initialTrend", so month 1''s rate is not spent: `scoredFrom` 1, 47 scored errors, and month 1 forecast at 1151.100000 with a residual of 2.300000. The level still starts at y_1, and month 0 still has no fitted value.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-holts-linear-trend', 10, 'Holt at alpha 0.5 and beta 0.2 ends EKENE-P1 at level 207.198880 and trend -5.798385. What does its h-step forecast look like?', '["The final level at every step, 207.198880, just as simple smoothing gives.", "A curve that flattens by a factor each step, reaching 184.933594 at step 12.", "l_n + b_n at every step, so 201.400495 repeated all the way to step 12.", "l_n + h b_n, a straight line: 201.400495 at step 1, 137.618258 at step 12."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Holt''s basis ends "forecast l_n + h b_n": every step falls by b_n, a straight line from 201.400495 at step 1 to 137.618258 at step 12. A flat forecast at the final level is simple smoothing''s. A forecast that flattens, 184.933594 at step 12, is the damped trend at phi 0.9. Holt multiplies the trend by h, so the steps differ.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-holts-linear-trend', 11, 'Holt fitted on EKENE-P5 ends on a trend of -0.702187 bbl/d per month. What does its h-step forecast do?', '["It stops at 0 from step 17 on, because the engine clips any negative rate.", "The call is refused at step 17 by naming `h`, since a rate cannot be negative.", "It crosses zero: 0.078646 at step 16, -0.623541 at step 17, returned as computed.", "It levels off near 11.100000, the last month''s rate, as the trend fades out."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Holt''s straight line falls by b_n every step and runs through zero between steps 16 and 17. The engine returns the line as computed; it does not clip or bend a forecast, and nothing in a forecast is refused. A trend that fades is the damped method, and Holt''s trend never fades past the last month.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-holts-linear-trend', 12, 'What is an honest way to report Holt''s EKENE-P5 forecast beyond step 16?', '["Replace the negative steps with 0 and present what is left as Holt''s forecast for the well.", "Report the step where the line reaches zero as a limit of the method, or shorten h.", "Quote step 17, -0.623541, as the rate the well will produce that month.", "Refit with a larger beta, again and again, until the line stays above zero."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The number is right for the method and wrong for the well, and the course names three honest responses: shorten h, report the zero crossing as a limit of the method, or use a method whose trend fades. Replacing steps with 0 presents an edit as the method''s output, a negative rate is no rate, and a given beta tuned to hide the crossing is a judgement dressed as a fit.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-holts-linear-trend', 13, 'In this course, what does the word trend name?', '["The smoothed trend state b of holt or damped, in bbl/d per month.", "The slope of a least-squares line drawn through all 48 monthly rates.", "Any direction in which the rates move over the last few months.", "The change in rate from the second-last month to the last, in percent."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course legislates trend as the smoothed state b of holt or damped, in bbl/d per month; Holt on EKENE-P5 ends on a trend of -0.702187 bbl/d per month. A regression slope through the rates is a different number, and any direction in conversation is the loose meaning the rule excludes. A percentage change between two months is not the state b.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-holts-linear-trend', 14, 'An `initialTrend` given as a word reaches a holt fit. What does the engine reply?', '["It treats the word as a blank and falls back on b_1 = y_2 - y_1.", "Refused by naming `beta`, the parameter that weights the trend.", "\"initialTrend applies to ''holt'' and ''damped'' only: ''ses'' has no trend\", naming `initialTrend`.", "It refuses it by name: \"initialTrend must be a finite number when given\"."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A given `initialTrend` must be a finite number, and the engine refuses anything else by naming the field, in its own words quoted in the key. A word is not read as a blank, so the start b_1 = y_2 - y_1 is not used in its place. The field named is the one at fault, and the other message about `initialTrend` is the refusal for an initial trend passed to ses.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-holts-linear-trend', 15, 'Why does holt need at least 3 months before the engine will fit it?', '["Its grid needs three points before it can place a trend between them.", "A slope is fitted by least squares, and a line needs at least three rates.", "The first two set the initial level and trend, and the third is the first scored forecast.", "Three months give one level and two trends, one each for alpha and beta."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s refusal on two months says so in its own words: "y has 2 values: ''holt'' needs at least 3 (the first two set the initial level and trend, the third is the first scored forecast)". The grid runs over parameter values and never over months; no least-squares line is fitted; and Holt carries a single trend state.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-damped-trend', 1, 'Where does the damped trend''s recursion differ from Holt''s?', '["phi takes alpha''s place in the level update, as the weight on each new rate.", "phi multiplies the level, so every fitted value shrinks by a fixed factor each month.", "phi multiplies the old trend, both in the fitted value and in the trend update.", "Only past the last month; inside the series the damped fit runs exactly as Holt''s."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The damped basis reads f_t = l_{t-1} + phi b_{t-1} and b_t = beta (l_t - l_{t-1}) + (1 - beta) phi b_{t-1}: phi appears in exactly the two places the trend is carried. The level update, with alpha as its weight, is Holt''s unchanged. phi never touches the level, and because it acts inside the series the damped fit''s final state differs from Holt''s.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-damped-trend', 2, 'At alpha 0.5, beta 0.2 and phi 0.9 on EKENE-P1, step 1 changes by -3.459147 and step 2 by -3.113233. What rule links them?', '["Each change is phi times the one before: -3.113233 is 0.9 of -3.459147.", "The final trend b_n, -3.843497, repeated as the change at every step.", "Alpha times the change before, so 0.5 of the previous change at every step of the list.", "Every step moves the forecast 10 percent of the way to zero."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The damped h-step forecast adds phi^h b_n at step h, so each change is phi times the change before; the ratio column reads 0.900000 at every step, checked to 1.00e-9. A change equal to b_n at every step is Holt''s straight line. alpha weights the level update and plays no part past the last month, and nothing in the rule pulls the forecast toward zero.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-damped-trend', 3, 'On the same fit, with final level 209.755413 and final trend -3.843497, what is the first change of the damped forecast?', '["b_n itself, -3.843497, with damping starting only from step 2.", "phi squared times b_n, since step 1 already counts as one damping.", "The last month''s residual, multiplied by phi to give the first step.", "phi b_n, 0.9 times -3.843497, which is -3.459147."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Step 1 of the damped forecast is l_n + phi b_n, so the first change from the final level is phi b_n: 0.9 times -3.843497 is -3.459147. The damping acts from step 1, the power of phi at step 1 is 1, and no residual enters the h-step forecast.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-damped-trend', 4, 'Where does the damped forecast at phi 0.9 on EKENE-P1 head as h grows?', '["Down by b_n every step without end, just as Holt''s straight line does.", "Toward l_n + b_n phi / (1 - phi), 175.163940, which step 400 matches to 1.00e-9.", "Toward zero, since a damped forecast of any declining well ends at 0.", "Back to its final level l_n, 209.755413, once the trend has faded away completely."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The bracket phi + phi^2 + ... + phi^h approaches phi / (1 - phi), so the forecast approaches l_n + b_n phi / (1 - phi), 175.163940; the engine''s step 400 is 175.163940, the limit to 1.00e-9. Falling by b_n every step is Holt. The limit is a finite drop below l_n, so the forecast neither reaches zero here nor returns to 209.755413.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-damped-trend', 5, 'The damped forecast is 184.933594 at step 12 and 175.163940 at step 400. What do the two figures show?', '["The forecast levels off at a rate, and at step 12 it is still above its limit.", "They are one limit written at two precisions, reached from step 12 on and held.", "The forecast climbs again after step 12, once phi takes over from the final trend.", "Step 400 has been clipped at the lowest rate the series ever recorded."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A damped forecast adds ever smaller changes and approaches its limit, 175.163940, which step 400 matches; at step 12 it is 184.933594 and still falling toward it. Both figures print to six decimals and differ, so they are two values. The changes all share the sign of b_n, so the forecast never climbs, and the engine clips nothing.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-damped-trend', 6, 'With phi left out, which values does the fit search for it?', '["0 to 1, the same box the fit searches for alpha and beta.", "Any value above 0 and at most 1, the rule for a given phi.", "0.85 to 0.95, the inner points of the coarse phi grid only.", "0.8 to 0.98 inclusive, the stated PHI_MIN and PHI_MAX."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A fitted phi is searched from 0.8 to 0.98 inclusive, the defaults `PHI_MIN` and `PHI_MAX`. The box 0 to 1 is alpha''s and beta''s. Above 0 and at most 1 is the rule for a phi that is given and held fixed. The grid''s points run 0.8, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95, 0.98, and the compass search works across the whole range.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-damped-trend', 7, 'Which values of phi may a caller give and have held fixed?', '["Only 0.8 to 0.98, the fitted range.", "Any number from 0 to 1 inclusive, so phi 0 is allowed as well.", "Any number above 0 and at most 1, phi 0.5 included.", "Any positive number, since a phi above 1 only steepens the trend."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A given phi may be any number above 0 and at most 1, so phi 0.5 is accepted on EKENE-P1 and held fixed. The range 0.8 to 0.98 applies only when phi is fitted. phi 0 and phi 1.05 are both refused, in one sentence that states both rules.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-damped-trend', 8, 'Which message comes back when a caller gives the damped method phi 1.05?', '["\"phi applies to ''damped'' only: ''holt'' is the damped method with phi = 1\"", "\"phi must be a number above 0 and at most 1 when given (when fitted it is searched from 0.8 to 0.98)\"", "A fit with phi moved down to 0.98, the top of the fitted range, and a warning attached to the result.", "\"phi must be a number from 0 to 1 (inclusive)\", naming `phi`."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine refuses a given phi of 0 or of 1.05 with the message in the key, its own words, which state the rule for a given phi and for a fitted one together. The message about holt is the refusal for a phi passed to holt. A refusal returns no fit, so nothing is moved to 0.98, and the inclusive 0 to 1 wording is the alpha and beta rule.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-damped-trend', 9, 'Fitted on EKENE-P4, the damped trend ends at phi 0.800000 with `atBounds` listing phi = 0.8. What does that tell you?', '["The SSE was still falling at the lower edge: the strongest damping the fit may choose.", "The search failed to converge and returned its starting grid point unchanged.", "EKENE-P4 has no trend, so the engine set phi to its default value of 0.8.", "Someone gave phi as 0.8, since `atBounds` lists each parameter a caller gives."]'::jsonb, 0, '`atBounds` lists a fitted parameter that ended exactly on a bound of its box. phi on 0.8 is a fitted value like any other: the SSE was still falling at the edge, so on this noisy well the fit wanted the trend to fade as fast as the box allows. Every fit in this course converged, phi has no default when fitted, and a given parameter is listed in `fixed`, never in `atBounds`.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-damped-trend', 10, 'On EKENE-P1 phi 0.5 is given, and alpha and beta are left out. What does the fit record show?', '["`atBounds` lists phi = 0.5, since that value lies below the fitted range.", "A refusal, since 0.5 lies outside the range 0.8 to 0.98 a phi must keep.", "`fixed` lists phi, alpha and beta, since a given phi fixes all three parameters.", "`fixed` lists phi; `atBounds` lists beta = 1 and leaves the given phi out."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A given phi 0.5 is accepted and held, so `fixed` lists it; alpha and beta are fitted around it, to 0.784159 and 1.000000, and the fitted beta on its upper bound appears in `atBounds` as beta = 1. A given parameter is never listed in `atBounds`, 0.8 to 0.98 applies only to a fitted phi, and alpha and beta stayed free.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-damped-trend', 11, 'The damped trend is run with phi 1 given, alpha 0.5 and beta 0.2, on EKENE-P1. What comes back?', '["A refusal, because a given phi has to lie strictly below 1.", "The figures of simple smoothing, since phi 1 switches the trend off.", "Holt''s SSE, 29230.297102, and Holt''s 12 forecasts, bit for bit.", "Figures close to Holt''s that part from them in the last decimals."]'::jsonb, 2, 'With phi 1 every phi in the recursion multiplies by 1 and Holt remains: the engine returns the same SSE, 29230.297102, and the same 12 forecasts as Holt at the same alpha and beta, bit for bit. A given phi may be 1. phi 1 keeps the whole trend, and the figures are identical, a stronger claim than close.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-damped-trend', 12, 'A phi is passed with the method ''holt''. Why does the engine refuse it?', '["It has no trend, so there is nothing for any phi to damp.", "Holt is the damped method with phi = 1, so it takes no phi of its own.", "Only a phi from 0.8 to 0.98 is accepted by holt, and the value passed lay outside.", "A given phi would block the search, and holt needs phi fitted."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s own words are "phi applies to ''damped'' only: ''holt'' is the damped method with phi = 1". Having no trend to damp is the reason given when phi reaches simple smoothing. Holt accepts no phi at any value, fitted or given.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-damped-trend', 13, 'On EKENE-P5, Holt''s straight line is below zero by step 17. What does the damped trend fitted on the same well forecast at step 24?', '["6.569332 bbl/d, still above zero.", "-0.623541 bbl/d, crossing zero a step behind the Holt line.", "0.078646 bbl/d, its last step above zero before it crosses.", "7.425050 bbl/d, the rate it holds without change from step 12 on."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The damped trend fitted on EKENE-P5 forecasts 6.569332 bbl/d at step 24, where Holt''s line is already below zero. -0.623541 and 0.078646 are Holt''s steps 17 and 16. 7.425050 is the damped forecast at step 12; the damped forecast keeps falling toward its limit, so it holds no rate unchanged.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-damped-trend', 14, 'Fitted on EKENE-P2, the damped trend lists `atBounds` alpha = 1, beta = 0. What do those two bounds describe?', '["A fit that never ran, so the corner of the grid came back with its starting values left unchanged.", "The shut-in months were dropped before fitting, leaving alpha and beta undefined on the well.", "The caller gave alpha 1 and beta 0, which the engine records in `atBounds` for every fit.", "A level that jumps to each new rate, and a trend that learns nothing new and only fades by phi."]'::jsonb, 3, 'alpha 1 puts all the weight on the newest rate, and beta 0 means the trend takes nothing from the newest change in level, fading only by phi 0.928195 each month. Both are fitted values on a bound, and the fit converged. The shut-in zeros are rates the fit reads, and a given parameter would appear in `fixed`.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-damped-trend', 15, 'Why is a fitted phi held to 0.8 to 0.98?', '["The compass search cannot step below 0.8 without raising a warning about its step size.", "The range follows FPP3, keeping a fitted damped trend distinct from simple smoothing and from Holt.", "Wells never decline with damping weaker than 0.98, a figure taken from decline curve theory.", "A phi below 0.8 gives a negative forecast on every declining well in the Ekene field."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine follows Forecasting: Principles and Practice (FPP3), which restricts an estimated phi to 0.8 to 0.98: far below, the trend fades so fast the method is close to simple smoothing, and near 1 it is close to Holt. The range sets the search''s box and the search works inside it; no decline theory fixes 0.98; and for the same final trend a lower phi makes the drop to the limit, b_n phi / (1 - phi), smaller, which keeps the forecast nearer the final level.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-fitting-the-parameters', 1, 'A caller supplies phi for the damped method and supplies nothing for alpha or beta. How are the three parameters treated?', '["All three are fitted, the given phi serving only as the search''s start.", "It holds phi fixed, lists it in `fixed`, and fits alpha and beta.", "The call is refused, since the damped method fits all three or none of them.", "alpha and beta held at 0.5 and 0.2."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A parameter given is held fixed and a parameter left out is fitted, so a caller can give some and leave others; the result lists the given ones in `fixed`. On EKENE-P1, phi 0.5 given fits alpha and beta around it. No start is taken from a given value, nothing is refused, and 0.5 and 0.2 are values a lesson chose to give, never a default.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-fitting-the-parameters', 2, 'What does the fit minimise when a parameter is left free?', '["The error of the step 12 forecast measured against the last month''s rate.", "The number of fitted parameters that end on a bound and appear in `atBounds`.", "A sum of absolute one-step errors, so that large misses count no more than small ones.", "The SSE of the scored one-step errors."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis states the target: "free parameters minimise the one-step SSE over the scored errors". The h-step forecasts play no part in the fit, `atBounds` is a report the fit makes afterwards, and squaring makes large errors count much more than small ones.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-fitting-the-parameters', 3, 'How many coarse grid points does the fit score for holt, both parameters free?', '["121, eleven alpha values by eleven beta values.", "11, since beta is searched only once alpha has been fixed by the grid.", "605, the full grid of alpha, beta and the five values of phi together.", "38, the same number of SSE evaluations the ses fit takes on EKENE-P1."]'::jsonb, 0, 'alpha and beta each run 0 to 1 by 0.1, eleven values, and holt scores every pair: 121 points. The grid of 605 adds the five phi values and is the damped method''s. 11 points is simple smoothing''s grid, and 38 is the total of the ses fit''s grid and compass search on EKENE-P1.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-fitting-the-parameters', 4, 'Two coarse grid points score the same SSE. Which one does the fit keep?', '["The later one, which replaces the best on any SSE that is equal or lower.", "The one lying nearer the centre of the box, where alpha and beta are 0.5.", "The earlier one, in the order alpha outermost, then beta, then phi.", "Both, with the search starting from their average."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis says "ties keep the earlier point", and the grid is scored alpha outermost, then beta, then phi, so the loop order is part of the rule. A later point replaces the best only when its SSE is lower by the stated relative margin. No point is preferred for its place in the box, and the search starts from one point.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-fitting-the-parameters', 5, 'When does a later grid point replace the best one found so far?', '["Any equal or lower SSE replaces it.", "When its SSE is below the best''s SSE x (1 - 1.00e-12).", "Only if its SSE is lower by at least 0.05 of the best''s value, a margin the grid states.", "If its SSE is lower and its alpha higher."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tie rule is relative: a later point replaces the best only when its SSE is below best x (1 - 1.00e-12), the stated `GRID_TIE_REL`, so an equal SSE keeps the earlier point. 0.05 is the compass search''s first step, a fraction of each range, and alpha plays no part in the rule.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-fitting-the-parameters', 6, 'What does the compass search do after a whole sweep that improves nothing, while its step is still above 2^-30 of the range?', '["Stops at once, since no trial in that sweep lowered the SSE at all.", "Doubles its step, so that it can climb out of a small local dip.", "Goes back to the grid and restarts from the second best grid point.", "Halves its step and sweeps again."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The search tries the step added and taken away on each free parameter in turn, moves to the best trial that lowers the SSE, and halves the step after a sweep that improves nothing. It stops only when a sweep at a step of at most 2^-30 of the range improves nothing, so a step still above that is halved. It never doubles its step or returns to the grid.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-fitting-the-parameters', 7, 'How large is the compass search''s first step?', '["0.05 of each parameter''s range, half the grid spacing.", "0.1, the grid spacing.", "2^-30 of the range, the finest step, widened as the search proceeds.", "0.8, the lowest fitted phi."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The first step is 0.05 of each parameter''s range, the stated `PS_STEP`, half the grid''s spacing of 0.1, so the search begins by looking between grid points. 2^-30 of the range is where the step ends, and the step only ever halves. 0.8 is the lower bound of a fitted phi.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-fitting-the-parameters', 8, 'Fitting ses on EKENE-P1, the grid starts at alpha 1 and the search makes 0 moves in 38 SSE evaluations. Why no moves?', '["A round grid value of alpha, such as 1, makes the engine skip the search.", "The SSE surface is flat there, so every alpha scores 49562.030000 alike.", "Alpha 1 is on the edge of the box, and a lower alpha raises the SSE.", "The compass search runs only for holt and damped."]'::jsonb, 2, 'From alpha 1 the search tries a higher alpha, which the box does not allow, and a lower one, which raises the SSE, so no trial improves: it halves its step until it stops. Of the 38 evaluations, 11 are the grid and the rest are the search''s trials, so the search did run. 49562.030000 is the SSE at alpha 1 alone; lower alphas score more.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-fitting-the-parameters', 9, 'Why is the final SSE of a fit never above the grid SSE?', '["A rescoring of the grid keeps the lower SSE.", "The search moves only to a trial that lowers the SSE.", "Its search works on a finer grid that contains every coarse grid point.", "The final SSE is rounded down to 0.1."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The compass search moves to the best trial only when that trial lowers the SSE, so it only ever moves downhill from the grid''s best point: the final SSE is at most the grid SSE on every method. No rescoring, finer grid or rounding takes place.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-fitting-the-parameters', 10, 'What does `atBounds` list in a fit record?', '["Every fitted parameter that ended exactly on an edge of its box.", "Every parameter the caller gave, which the engine holds fixed through the fit.", "Every parameter lying within 0.05 of an edge, the first compass step.", "Each grid choice the search left unmoved."]'::jsonb, 0, '`atBounds` lists every fitted parameter that ended exactly on a bound: alpha and beta at 0 or 1, phi at 0.8 or 0.98. Given parameters are listed in `fixed` and never in `atBounds`. The list asks for exactly the bound, and whether the search moved a parameter is a separate record.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-fitting-the-parameters', 11, 'Holt is fitted on the exact line 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, 20, 22, 24, where every alpha and beta give SSE 0. What does the fit return?', '["A refusal naming `y`, because an SSE that is already 0 leaves nothing to minimise.", "alpha 1 and beta 1, both on their upper bounds, as a naive trend would take them.", "alpha 0.5 and beta 0.5, the centre of the box, which the fit picks to break the tie.", "alpha 0 and beta 0, the grid''s first point, after 0 moves: the parameters are not identified."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Golden `holt-linear-exact` scores SSE 0 at every point, so the grid tie keeps its first point, alpha 0 and beta 0, and the search makes 0 moves. Any pair would fit as well: the parameters are not identified. The forecasts 26.000000, 28.000000, 30.000000 continue the line. The engine refuses nothing here and breaks ties by order, never by position.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-fitting-the-parameters', 12, 'When does the compass search stop with `converged` true?', '["When the SSE falls below 1.00e-12, the stated relative tie band of the grid.", "As soon as 200000 SSE evaluations have been spent on the one fit.", "When a sweep at a step of at most 2^-30 of each range improves nothing.", "After exactly 26 halvings, whatever size the step has reached."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The stop rule is on the step: when a sweep at a step of at most 2^-30 of each range improves nothing, the search stops with `converged` true and `finalStep` records the fraction. 1.00e-12 is the grid''s tie band. Reaching 200000 evaluations first stops the search with `converged` false. The fits on EKENE-P1 each made 26 halvings because that is how many take 0.05 below 2^-30.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-fitting-the-parameters', 13, 'What happens if a search reaches 200000 SSE evaluations before its step rule is met?', '["The call is refused by naming the parameter the search was working on.", "It stops with `converged` false and a warning, and still returns its parameters.", "It restarts from the grid with a finer step and carries on searching.", "It returns no forecast at all, only the grid''s best point and its SSE."]'::jsonb, 1, '`PS_MAX_EVALS` is 200000; a fit that reaches it first stops with `converged` false and `warnings`, and the parameters and forecasts still come back. A warning is attached to a result, so there is no refusal and no restart. No fit in this course reaches that cap.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-fitting-the-parameters', 14, 'Every parameter of a holt fit is given. What does the fit record hold?', '["`optimiser` is null, and the basis reads \"all parameters given: no estimation\".", "An `optimiser` record of 0 moves, beside the basis \"all parameters given: no estimation\".", "The grid''s 121 SSE values, kept for comparison with the given pair.", "`converged` false, since no search ran to meet the stop rule."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With nothing left to fit, no search runs, the optimiser record is null and the basis reads "all parameters given: no estimation". There is no optimiser record of moves to read and no `converged`, true or false, and no grid is scored.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-fitting-the-parameters', 15, 'Why does the engine fit by a grid and a compass search?', '["Maximum likelihood with a gradient optimiser is what it runs, as other tools do.", "Only this search finds the least SSE anywhere in the box, which no other method can.", "Random starts are drawn, and averaging many fits steadies the parameters.", "It is deterministic: the same series gives the same parameters on any machine."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course states the choice: least one-step SSE, a grid then a compass search in a stated box, chosen because it is deterministic, the same series giving the same parameters. Maximum likelihood with a gradient optimiser is the common alternative. `converged` true is a statement about the search, never a promise of the least SSE anywhere in the box, and the search uses no random start.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-well-forecast', 1, 'In what order does the Associate workflow run on one well?', '["Pick the method with the flattest forecast, fit it, then read the series for gaps.", "Fit only the damped trend, since it always scores best, and report its SSE.", "Compare the methods by SSE first, then fit whichever one scored the lowest.", "Read the series, fit every method, compare by MSE, then report the forecast."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The workflow reads the series first, so a missing month is caught before any fit, then fits each method with every parameter free, compares by MSE because the methods score different numbers of errors, and reports with the method, parameters, months and h. No method always scores best, and there is no SSE to compare until the methods have been fitted.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-well-forecast', 2, 'All three methods are fitted on EKENE-P4 with every parameter free. Which has the lowest in-sample MSE?', '["damped, at 4502.618003.", "ses, at 4585.145790, the lowest of the three once the SSE is divided.", "holt, at 6048.241985, since a straight trend follows any decline best.", "ses, since its SSE of 215501.852140 is spread over one more error."]'::jsonb, 0, 'By MSE the damped trend is lowest at 4502.618003, ses next at 4585.145790 and holt highest at 6048.241985. Dividing ses''s SSE by its 47 errors gives 4585.145790, still above the damped MSE.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-well-forecast', 3, 'What does the lowest in-sample MSE on EKENE-P4 tell the forecaster?', '["Which method will forecast the next twelve months of EKENE-P4 with the smallest error.", "That the damped forecast at step 12, 239.235397, is the rate EKENE-P4 will produce.", "Which method best followed months the fit had already seen, and nothing yet about months to come.", "That the other two methods are wrong for this well and can be dropped from any report."]'::jsonb, 2, 'An in-sample MSE scores one-step forecasts over months the fit had already seen, with parameters chosen on those months. Whether a method forecasts months it has not seen is a separate question, answered by scoring forecasts on months held back, which is the Professional tier''s work. Until then the ranking is a statement about the history.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-well-forecast', 4, 'On EKENE-P4 the three fitted methods give step 12 forecasts of 241.813456, 172.008815 and 239.235397. Which is Holt''s?', '["241.813456, which is also Holt''s step 1 forecast on the same fit.", "172.008815, the one that falls by its final trend at every step.", "239.235397, which stays close to its step 1 figure of 244.373345.", "None of them, since Holt is fitted here without a trend."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Holt''s straight line falls from 236.028094 at step 1 to 172.008815 at step 12. 241.813456 at both steps is the flat ses forecast. 239.235397, beside 244.373345 at step 1, is the damped trend with phi on 0.8, fading fast. Holt always carries a trend, here fitted with beta 0.354191.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-well-forecast', 5, 'A write-up of an Associate forecast ends with a line about its numbers. What does that line say?', '["That they are accurate, since every fit converged with `converged` true.", "That the method with the lowest MSE has been proved the right one for the well.", "Nothing about testing, because a converged fit needs no further test.", "That they are in-sample until the forecast is tested on months the fit never saw."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The write-up names the well and months, the method, each parameter fitted or given with any bound, `scoredFrom` and the MSE, h, and that the numbers are in-sample until tested. `converged` is a statement about the search, never about forecasting, and a lowest in-sample MSE proves nothing about months to come.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-well-forecast', 6, 'How should a write-up report the phi of the damped trend fitted on EKENE-P4?', '["Phi 0.8, given by the forecaster to damp the trend.", "Only as 0.800000, since a bound is a detail only of the optimiser.", "As phi 0.800000, fitted, on its lower bound.", "As phi 0.98, the far edge the search was heading toward."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The phi was fitted and ended on the lower bound 0.8, which `atBounds` lists, and the write-up says so beside the value so a reader knows the fit ran out of room. Nothing was given, so calling it a choice misleads. The fit ran toward the lower edge, 0.8, where the damping is strongest.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-well-forecast', 7, 'Why does the engine reproduce the NIST handbook''s figures on its 12-point series at alpha 0.1?', '["Any recursion rounds to the handbook''s printed figures, whatever start it happens to use.", "The handbook uses the same start, l_1 = y_1, and the same MSE divisor, the scored errors.", "The handbook fits alpha by the same grid and compass search that this engine runs.", "Both divide the MSE by 12, the number of points in the handbook''s short series."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The first NIST case starts the level at the first observation and divides the MSE by the scored errors, as this engine does; same start, same divisor, same recursion, so the figures agree. A different start moves the early fitted values and the MSE. Alpha 0.1 is given in that case, so no search runs, and the divisor is the scored errors, which excludes the first point.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-well-forecast', 8, 'The handbook prints an MSE of 19.0 where the engine gives 18.983492. How do the two compare?', '["They agree at the one decimal the handbook prints, which is all it claims.", "No: the engine''s lower figure shows its start differs from the handbook''s own start.", "Only once alpha is refitted do they agree.", "They disagree, because the handbook divides by 11 while the engine divides by 12."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A published check compares to the printed precision and never beyond it: 18.983492 rounds to 19.0 at one decimal. The start is the same, l_1 = y_1, alpha 0.1 is given in that case, and the engine''s divisor is the scored errors, the same as the handbook''s.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-well-forecast', 9, 'In the handbook''s double smoothing example the engine fits alpha 0.362309 and a trend weight of 1.000000. What does the golden give the engine so that it matches?', '["phi 0.8 given, which damps the handbook''s trend until the two sets of figures agree.", "Nothing extra: the start b_1 = y_2 - y_1 is the handbook''s own start for the trend as well.", "beta given as 1, since a fitted beta never reaches its upper bound.", "The handbook''s own start for the trend, b_1 = 0.8, given as `initialTrend`."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The handbook uses its own start for the trend, b_1 = 0.8, and the golden gives it to the engine as `initialTrend`; to reproduce another tool, match its start first. The fitted beta ends on its upper bound and `atBounds` lists beta = 1, so it was fitted. No phi is involved in Holt''s method.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-well-forecast', 10, 'What does the engine''s agreement with the NIST handbook establish?', '["Future months will bear out the engine''s forecasts of the Ekene wells, it shows.", "The course''s own oracle is no longer needed to check the engine''s arithmetic.", "That the recursion, the start and the MSE divisor mean what the published literature means.", "That the grid and compass search find the least SSE on every series in every box."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A handbook written by other people for other purposes is unlikely to share a misunderstanding with the engine, so agreement checks the arithmetic, the start and the divisor, and on the two fitted cases the search''s answer on a short series. It says nothing about forecasting skill, it adds to the oracle''s check without replacing it, and it covers only the handbook''s cases.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-well-forecast', 11, 'A task asks for the MSE of a Holt fit on a 48-month series with alpha and beta given. What does the SSE get divided by?', '["48, the months in the series, month 0 included.", "46, the scored errors, since Holt spends month 1 on the start.", "47, the scored errors of simple smoothing on the same 48 months of rates.", "12, the number of steps a forecast usually covers."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The MSE divides the SSE by the scored errors only, and Holt''s `scoredFrom` is 2, so 48 months give 46 errors; on EKENE-P1 at alpha 0.5 and beta 0.2 that is 29230.297102 over 46, 635.441241. Dividing by the months gives a different, wrong figure, 47 is simple smoothing''s count, and the forecast steps are never scored in-sample.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-well-forecast', 12, 'A task asks for Holt''s fitted beta on EKENE-P1. Which figure answers it?', '["0.381513, fitted with alpha left free as well.", "0.2, the beta given beside alpha 0.5 in the teaching fit.", "0.353869, the beta the damped trend fits on the same well.", "1.000000, the beta fitted when phi 0.5 is given to damped."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Holt fitted on EKENE-P1 with both parameters free ends at alpha 0.661937 and beta 0.381513. 0.2 is a given value, which answers a different question. 0.353869 is the damped fit''s beta, and 1.000000 is the beta fitted around a given phi 0.5 on the damped method.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-well-forecast', 13, 'A forecast note says the damped forecast of EKENE-P4 is accurate. What does the course''s vocabulary require of that word?', '["Nothing more, since the word is plain English and every reader takes it the same way.", "A figure within 10 percent of the last month''s rate, the test the course applies.", "The fitted phi, since accuracy in this course is the amount of damping chosen.", "A named metric on named months; at this tier, the in-sample MSE over the scored months."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Accuracy in this course names its metric and its months; at the Associate tier the only metric is the in-sample MSE over the scored months of the fit. The course sets no percentage test, and phi is a parameter of the method, never a measure of how well it fits.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-well-forecast', 14, 'Which phrase uses the word forecast as the course legislates it?', '["\"Holt''s forecast for month 2, 1130.700000\", a value from inside the series.", "\"the forecast of the field\", with no method attached.", "\"damped, step 12, 239.235397\", a point forecast named with its method.", "\"a forecast of about 240\", rounded."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A forecast is the point forecast of one fitted method, named with its method; a one-step forecast inside the series is a fitted value, so Holt''s 1130.700000 for month 2 is a fitted value. A forecast with no method cannot be checked, and a rounded figure with no method, well or step is not one the engine returned.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-well-forecast', 15, 'In a damped fit on EKENE-P3, the forecaster gave phi 0.9. What does the write-up do about it?', '["Reports phi as fitted, since the other two parameters of the method were fitted.", "Says phi was given and why, as the engine''s `fixed` list records it.", "Leaves phi out, since a given value is not a result of the fit and needs no line.", "Reports it in `atBounds`, since 0.9 lies inside the fitted range."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A given parameter is a judgement, and the write-up says it was given and why; the engine lists every given parameter in `fixed`. Calling it fitted misreports the work, leaving it out makes the forecast impossible to reproduce, and `atBounds` lists only fitted parameters on an edge of the box.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'Over months 20 to 27 EKENE-P2 reads 439.800000, 364.000000, 0.000000, 0.000000, 0.000000, 456.100000, 469.700000, 442.300000. Which is the first month back on production after the shut-in?', '["The last of the zero months, month 24.", "Month 25, at 456.100000 bbl/d.", "Month 26, since a restart month is never counted as producing.", "Only month 22, where the rate first reads 0."]'::jsonb, 1, 'EKENE-P2''s shut-in is months 22 to 24, counted from 0, at rate 0, and month 25 is 456.100000, the first month after it. Month 24 is still a zero month, month 26 reads 469.700000, and month 22 is where the shut-in begins.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'A caller passes the text ''EKENE-P1'' where the series of rates belongs. What does the engine reply?', '["It looks the well up by name and fits its 48 months.", "A refusal naming `method`, since no method can read a well name.", "\"y[0] must be a finite number: fill or drop missing values first\".", "\"y must be an array of numbers\", naming `y`."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine takes plain arrays and refuses a well name on the whole field `y`, in its own words "y must be an array of numbers". It holds no well data of its own to look up. The fault lies in `y`, so `method` is not named, and the index form of the message is for one bad value inside an array.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'A series of 100001 values is passed to `fitSmoothing`. What comes back?', '["A refusal in the engine''s words: \"y has 100001 values, above the 100000 this engine accepts\".", "The fit on the first 100000 values, with the value past the limit dropped from the end of the series.", "A fit on every value, since the limit of 100000 applies only to how many forecast steps h may ask for.", "A refusal naming `h`, in the words \"h must be a whole number from 0 to 10000\", because the series is long."]'::jsonb, 0, '`MAX_POINTS` is 100000, the most values a series may carry, and a longer series is refused by name. The engine trims nothing. The limit on h is a separate one, 10000, and the h message belongs to an h out of range.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'EKENE-P1 is passed with months 5 and 12 both set to null. Which field does the refusal name?', '["`y[12]`, the last gap, which the engine reports first.", "Both, as `y[5]` and `y[12]`, in one message.", "`y[5]`, the first index the engine meets.", "`y`, with a count of the two missing months."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A missing month is refused by name at the first index the engine meets, counting from 0, so the reply names `y[5]` alone. Filling or dropping month 5 is the caller''s decision, and the next call would then meet month 12. The engine does not count gaps or list them together.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'Month 10 of EKENE-P1 reads 734.800000, above month 9''s 705.100000, on a well in steady decline. What does the rise show?', '["A workover planted at month 10 that lifts the rate of every later month in the series.", "A monthly average carries noise, so the decline is not perfectly smooth.", "A data error the engine flags in `notes` before any fit.", "The start of a facility plateau held at that rate from then on."]'::jsonb, 1, 'EKENE-P1 was drawn from a hyperbolic decline with noise of 3 percent, so a single month can rise above the one before; a method has to decide how much of each month to believe. The workover is planted on EKENE-P2 and the plateau on EKENE-P3. The engine flags no data error in a fit; it refuses only a value that is not a finite number.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'A well''s history shows a clear yearly cycle in its rates. Which method in this engine models that cycle?', '["None: the engine builds no seasonal smoothing, so no method here models a yearly cycle.", "The damped trend, whose phi carries a seasonal swing forward from one year into the next.", "Holt''s linear trend, once beta is fitted on at least 24 months of history.", "Simple smoothing, provided alpha is given as 12 so the level follows each season."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s methods are simple exponential smoothing, Holt''s linear trend and the damped trend, all additive, and it builds no seasonal smoothing (Holt-Winters). phi damps a trend and carries no cycle, beta weights the trend whatever the length of the series, and alpha must lie from 0 to 1.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'Every result the engine returns carries a `basis` block. What is it for?', '["The random seed used to draw the fitted values.", "A list of the months the engine filled before fitting.", "It holds the answer to any refusal the call produced.", "It names the convention used, so the working can be printed."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every result carries a `basis` block naming its convention, such as the recursion, the start and the MSE divisor, so a reader can print and check the working. A fit draws no random numbers, the engine fills no month, and a refusal returns `error` and `field` with no result.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'The course legislates the word error. What does it mean here?', '["Forecast minus actual, so a positive error means the forecast was high.", "Any gap between two numbers, whichever way round it is taken.", "Actual minus forecast, so a positive error means the forecast was low.", "The squared difference, so every error in the course is positive."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course legislates error as actual minus forecast, and a residual is an in-sample one-step error: on EKENE-P1 at alpha 0.3 month 1''s residual is 1153.400000 less 1176.100000, -22.700000, a forecast that was high. Reversing the order flips every sign, and the squares belong to the SSE.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'What do simple smoothing, Holt''s linear trend and the damped trend have in common in this engine?', '["All three are multiplicative, scaling the level by a factor each month of the series.", "All three are additive: any trend adds to the level, and an error to the forecast.", "Every one of them carries a trend, and the trend is damped by phi in all three of them.", "They share one set of parameters, so a single alpha fits all three."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s three methods are all additive exponential smoothing, with no multiplicative form. Simple smoothing carries no trend at all, and only the damped method takes phi. Each method is fitted on its own, with its own free parameters.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'At alpha 0.3 on EKENE-P1, month 3 ends on level 1101.018100. What is month 4''s fitted value?', '["954.800000, month 4''s own rate, since the level has caught up with the series.", "1057.152670, the level month 4 ends on once its own rate has arrived.", "1142.083000, the level two months back, carried forward by alpha.", "1101.018100, since the fitted value is the level from the month before."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Simple smoothing forecasts f_t = l_{t-1}, so month 4''s fitted value is month 3''s level, 1101.018100, and its residual is 954.800000 less that, -146.218100. 1057.152670 is month 4''s level after its rate is taken in. 1142.083000 is month 2''s level, month 3''s fitted value.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'At alpha 0.9 given, EKENE-P1''s flat forecast is 211.447679, while the last month is 211.400000. Why so close?', '["Nearly all the weight is on the newest month, so the final level sits almost on the last rate.", "At alpha 0.9 the engine rounds the forecast to the last month''s rate within 0.1 bbl/d each step.", "The fit moved alpha to its bound, so the final level became exactly the naive forecast at 211.400000.", "A forecast at alpha 0.9 is the mean of the last few months, which happens to fall near month 47."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At alpha 0.9 each level is 0.9 of the new rate plus 0.1 of the old forecast, so the final level ends barely above the last rate, 211.447679 against 211.400000. Nothing is rounded, alpha 0.9 is given and held, so the forecast is not the naive 211.400000, and no plain mean is taken.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'At alpha 0.1 on EKENE-P1 the SSE is 1954345.253138. Which MSE does the engine report beside it?', '["1954345.253138, since at a given alpha the MSE equals the SSE.", "The SSE over all 48 months, month 0 counted as a zero error.", "41581.813897, the SSE over 47 scored errors.", "282.956937, the flat forecast that alpha 0.1 leaves at every step."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Simple smoothing scores 47 errors on 48 months, and the MSE is the SSE over the scored errors: 1954345.253138 over 47 is 41581.813897. A given alpha does not merge the two fields. Month 0 has no residual and is not counted. 282.956937 is the forecast, a rate, never an error measure.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'On EKENE-P1, alpha 1 gives an MSE of 1054.511277 and alpha 0.5 an MSE of 2772.800063. What does the lower MSE at alpha 1 say about the step 12 forecast?', '["That the step 12 forecast at alpha 1 will miss the well''s rate by less than 1054.511277 bbl/d.", "Nothing directly: the MSE scores one-step errors on months the fit had already seen.", "Alpha 1''s step 12 forecast, 211.400000, becomes the rate the well will produce.", "Alpha 0.5 is wrong for the well, and its forecast must be thrown away."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The MSE averages squared one-step residuals over the scored months of the fit, so it rewards following the history one month ahead. It knows nothing about step 12, which is forecast past the last month, and an MSE is in squared bbl/d, so it is no bound on a rate miss. Whether any forecast fares well on months to come is a later tier''s test.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'Among the five long Ekene wells under ses with a fitted alpha, where is the MSE largest?', '["EKENE-P4, at 4585.145790, since noise always raises the MSE most.", "EKENE-P3, at 1958.100426, since a plateau cannot be smoothed.", "EKENE-P5, at 1115.991064, since a steep decline lags the most.", "EKENE-P2, at 8466.172766, with a shut-in and a workover in its series."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The fitted ses MSEs are 1054.511277 on EKENE-P1, 8466.172766 on EKENE-P2, 1958.100426 on EKENE-P3, 4585.145790 on EKENE-P4 and 1115.991064 on EKENE-P5. EKENE-P2 is the highest: its drops to 0 and jump back after the workover are large one-step errors. The other three figures are real but lower.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'When does simple smoothing''s flat forecast earn its place on a well?', '["On a series with no persistent direction, such as a plateau or heavy noise around a steady rate.", "On any steady decline, where the naive forecast follows the fall best of all the methods offered.", "On a short series only, since the engine refuses holt and damped on any series under 12 months.", "Whenever its fitted alpha lands on the bound 1, since that proves the level is the right model."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A flat forecast says the method sees no direction, which is right on a plateau or on noise about a steady rate and wrong on a clear decline, where every step inherits the error. Holt fits on as few as 3 months, so no rule of twelve exists. alpha on its bound 1 says the fit wants more weight on the newest month than the box allows, which on a decline is a sign the series asks for a trend.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'At alpha 0.5 and beta 0.2, EKENE-P1''s month 3 level moves from 1104.650000 to 1040.970000 on an old trend of -27.910000. Which trend does month 3 end on?', '["The full change in level, since the trend takes each newest change whole.", "-27.910000, since the trend is held until the level settles again.", "-35.064000, 0.2 of the change in level plus 0.8 of the old trend.", "-40.772440, the trend EKENE-P1 reaches by month 5 of the same fit."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The trend update is b_t = beta (l_t - l_{t-1}) + (1 - beta) b_{t-1}: 0.2 times the change from 1104.650000 to 1040.970000 plus 0.8 times -27.910000 gives -35.064000, as the table prints. Taking the full change is beta 1, holding the trend is beta 0, and -40.772440 is the trend two months later.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'In Holt''s teaching fit on EKENE-P1, month 2''s residual is -52.100000 and month 5''s is -5.978400. What changed in between?', '["The engine refitted alpha and beta at month 5, once enough months of the series were in.", "The trend steepened to follow the decline, so the fitted value closed on the rate.", "Months 3 and 4 were left out of scoring, which shrinks every residual that comes after.", "The level was reset to the newest rate at month 5, as the naive forecast does."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The trend moves from -22.700000 at the start to -40.772440 by month 5, as each month surprises the method on the low side, so month 5''s fitted value, 940.178400, sits within a few bbl/d of the rate 934.200000. alpha and beta are given and held, every month from index 2 is scored, and alpha 0.5 keeps half of the old forecast in each level.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'Fitted on EKENE-P1, holt reaches SSE 23041.767516 and damped SSE 22288.217610. What happens to their order once each SSE becomes an MSE?', '["It reverses, since damped carries one parameter more and its MSE is penalised for it.", "No order survives, because holt divides by 47 errors and damped by 46 on the same months.", "Nothing can be said until both SSEs are divided by the 48 months of the series.", "It holds: both divide by 46 scored errors, so damped''s MSE is the lower one too."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course compares methods by MSE because ses scores 47 errors and holt and damped 46. Holt and damped both start at l_1 = y_1, b_1 = y_2 - y_1 and score from index 2, so on EKENE-P1''s 48 months both SSEs are divided by 46 and their order is kept. The MSE adds no penalty for parameters, holt scores 46 errors just as damped does, and the divisor is the scored errors, never the months.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'On EKENE-P4, Holt''s fit reaches SSE 278219.131326 and ses''s SSE 215501.852140, each over its own scored errors. Which MSEs does the engine report?', '["6048.241985 for holt over 46 errors, and 4585.145790 for ses over 47.", "4585.145790 for holt and 6048.241985 for ses, since ses scores the extra month.", "One MSE for both, because the two methods score the same months of EKENE-P4.", "Holt''s is the lower of the two, as it divides its SSE by one error more than ses."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The MSE is the SSE over the scored errors. Holt scores from index 2, so 278219.131326 over 46 is 6048.241985; ses scores from index 1, so 215501.852140 over 47 is 4585.145790. Swapping the two figures swaps the methods, the two methods score different numbers of months, and holt divides by one error fewer than ses, which leaves its larger SSE with the higher MSE.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'What unit goes with Holt''s final state b on EKENE-P5, -0.702187?', '["bbl/d, the same unit as the rate each month.", "Percent per month, a decline expressed as a share of the rate.", "bbl/d per month, the smoothed monthly change in level.", "Per month only, like the decline rate Di each well was drawn with."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The trend is the smoothed state b of holt or damped, in bbl/d per month: each step of Holt''s h-step forecast adds b_n to the rate. It is a change in rate per month, so it carries more than the unit of a rate; it is no percentage; and the Di a well was drawn with is a fraction of the rate per month, a different quantity.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'How many months does the damped trend need when an `initialTrend` is given?', '["3, exactly as Holt needs with the start taken from the series.", "2: the first sets the level, the second is the first scored forecast.", "1, since the given trend leaves nothing for the series to supply.", "4, one extra month to set phi alongside the level and trend."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With an `initialTrend` given, y_2 is not spent on the start, so the damped method needs 2 months; with 1 the engine refuses in its own words, "y has 1 value: ''damped'' with an initialTrend needs at least 2 (the first sets the level, the second is the first scored forecast)". Three is the rule without a given trend, and phi takes no month of its own.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'A forecast at phi 0.9 is handed over with step changes of -2.521718 at step 4 and -2.269547 at step 5. How can a reader check it is damped at that phi?', '["Subtract the changes: the gap should equal the final trend b_n.", "Add up the changes: together they must total the final level l_n of the fit.", "Check that every change equals b_n, as a damped forecast requires.", "Divide one change by the change before: the ratio should read 0.900000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each damped change is phi times the one before, so -2.269547 over -2.521718 reads 0.900000, and the course''s table shows that ratio at every step. A difference between changes, a sum of changes, or a constant change describe no rule of the damped forecast; a constant change is Holt''s straight line.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'At alpha 0.5 and beta 0.2 on EKENE-P1, Holt gives 137.618258 at step 12 and the damped trend at phi 0.9 gives 184.933594. What does the gap between them show?', '["How much of a twelve-month forecast rests on phi alone, alpha and beta held the same.", "That the damped forecast is right and Holt''s line is wrong for EKENE-P1 in the months ahead.", "That the damped fit ran on different alpha and beta values from the ones Holt''s fit used.", "How far each forecast sits from the rate EKENE-P1 will produce at step 12."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both fits share alpha 0.5 and beta 0.2, and only phi differs, so the gap shows how much of a year''s forecast rests on that one choice. Both are in-sample fits projected forward, so neither number is a test of which is right, and the months that would settle it have not happened.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'A fitted phi ends on 0.98 and `atBounds` lists it. What does that bound describe?', '["The strongest damping the fit may choose, close to simple smoothing.", "A phi given by the caller, since 0.98 is the default the engine uses.", "The weakest damping the fit may choose, close to Holt for many steps.", "A search that ran out of evaluations before phi could reach 1."]'::jsonb, 2, 'phi 0.98 is the top of the fitted range, so it damps least and the forecast stays near Holt''s line for many steps; the SSE was still falling at that edge. 0.8 is the strongest damping. A given parameter goes in `fixed`, and a fitted phi never reaches 1 because its box stops at 0.98.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'Damped fitted on EKENE-P3 ends at alpha 0.594062, beta 0.798138 and phi 0.913180. What does `atBounds` read?', '["phi = 0.8, since the plateau pulls phi to its strongest damping.", "none: every fitted parameter ended inside its box.", "alpha = 1, since every long well fits alpha on its upper bound.", "beta = 1, since 0.798138 is rounded up to the bound."]'::jsonb, 1, 'EKENE-P3''s damped fit lists `atBounds` none: alpha, beta and phi all ended inside their boxes. EKENE-P4 is the well with phi on 0.8. alpha 1 is simple smoothing''s fit on four long wells, and the damped EKENE-P2 fit; the engine rounds no parameter to a bound.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'On EKENE-P1 the damped trend at phi 0.9 ends on final level 209.755413 and final trend -3.843497. How far below the final level does its limit lie?', '["b_n below it, at one step''s fall, so the limit is step 1''s 206.296266.", "No distance: a damped forecast settles on its final level once the trend fades.", "Twelve trends below it, since h 12 is where the damped fit stops falling.", "b_n phi / (1 - phi) below it, so the forecast settles at 175.163940."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The limit is l_n + b_n phi / (1 - phi); with a negative trend it sits a finite amount below the final level, 175.163940 here, matched by step 400. 206.296266 is step 1. The forecast keeps falling after step 1 and after step 12, 184.933594, so it neither holds the final level nor stops at step 12.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'Why does the course say a phi below 0.8 can only be given?', '["The fit searches phi from 0.8 to 0.98, while a given phi may be any value above 0 and at most 1.", "The engine refuses every phi below 0.8, given or fitted, with the message on phi''s range.", "A phi below 0.8 turns the damped method into simple smoothing, which takes no phi.", "Only the grid can reach below 0.8, and a caller who gives phi bypasses the grid."]'::jsonb, 0, 'No fit returns a phi under 0.8, because the search box for phi runs from 0.8 to 0.98. A caller may still hand the engine any phi above 0 and at most 1, and on EKENE-P1 a phi of 0.5 is accepted and held. At any phi the method stays the damped trend, and the coarse grid''s phi values all sit inside 0.8 to 0.98.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'Which list holds the coarse grid''s values of phi?', '["0 to 1 by 0.1, eleven values.", "0.8 to 0.98 in steps of 0.05, the first compass step.", "0.8, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95 and 0.98.", "0.5, 0.9 and 1, the values the lessons give."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The stated default `GRID_PHI` is 0.8, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95, 0.98. 0 to 1 by 0.1 is the grid of alpha and beta. The compass search works between grid points, and 0.5, 0.9 and 1 are phi values a lesson gave.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'Fitted on EKENE-P1, Holt''s grid starts at alpha 0.7 and beta 0.3. Where does the compass search take it?', '["Nowhere: the grid point is the minimum, so the search makes 0 moves and returns alpha 0.7, beta 0.3.", "To alpha 0.661937 and beta 0.381513 in 25 moves, the SSE falling from 23147.088556 to 23041.767516.", "To alpha 1 and beta 0, a corner of the box, which `atBounds` lists as the fit''s two bounds on this well.", "To alpha 0.657029 and beta 0.353869 in 50 moves, the SSE falling to 22288.217610."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The EKENE-P1 table shows Holt''s grid start alpha 0.7, beta 0.3 at SSE 23147.088556, and 25 moves to alpha 0.661937, beta 0.381513 at SSE 23041.767516. 0 moves is the ses row. alpha 1 and beta 0 are the damped EKENE-P2 bounds. 0.657029, 0.353869 and 50 moves are the damped fit''s, with phi also free.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'The damped fit on EKENE-P1 takes 1067 SSE evaluations. How many of them are the coarse grid?', '["605, the product 11 x 11 x 5 of the alpha, beta and phi grid values.", "121, since phi is left to the compass search and never placed on the grid.", "1067, since every evaluation of this fit is a grid point.", "38, the same count of grid points as the ses fit takes on EKENE-P1 in all."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Scoring every combination on the damped grid costs 605 evaluations; the rest of the 1067 are compass search trials between grid points. Dropping phi from that grid would leave Holt''s 121, and the ses fit on EKENE-P1 spends only 38 evaluations in total.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'Holt on EKENE-P1 ends with a `finalStep` of 7.45e-10. How does that figure relate to the stop rule?', '["Below 1.00e-12, the relative tie band, which is the figure a converged search has to reach.", "The SSE left at the end of the search, 7.45e-10 of the SSE the grid started from.", "It is how far alpha moved on the last sweep, a figure showing that the search had nearly finished.", "It is at most 2^-30 of the range, 9.31e-10, so a sweep at that step improving nothing met the rule."]'::jsonb, 3, '`finalStep` is the fraction of each range the step had reached; the search stops, `converged` true, when a sweep at a step of at most 2^-30, 9.31e-10, improves nothing, and 7.45e-10 is within it after 26 halvings from 0.05. 1.00e-12 is the grid''s tie band, and `finalStep` is a step size, never an SSE or a move.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'Each of the three fits on EKENE-P1 makes 26 halvings, though their moves run from 0 to 50. Why do the halvings agree?', '["The engine fixes the number of halvings at 26 for every fit, whatever the series is.", "Each move resets the step, and every fit happens to make 26 of those resets.", "Halvings count the step down from 0.05 to below 2^-30; moves happen at whatever step improves.", "26 halvings are what 200000 evaluations allow before the search is capped."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The step starts at 0.05 of each range and halves after each sweep that improves nothing, and it takes 26 halvings to fall from 0.05 to below 2^-30; a move keeps the step. The count is not a fixed setting, and no fit here comes near the cap of 200000 evaluations, the damped fit taking 1067.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'Holt''s fitted alpha and beta are given back to the engine as fixed values, at full precision. What returns?', '["A fresh search from those values, reaching a slightly lower SSE.", "The same SSE and forecasts, bit for bit, with `optimiser` null.", "A refusal, since fitted values cannot be passed back as given.", "The grid''s best point, since a given pair is checked against the grid."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Giving the fitted parameters back as fixed values returns the same SSE and forecasts bit for bit, and with nothing left to fit the optimiser record is null. No search runs, nothing is refused, and no grid is scored. It is a way to freeze a fit and show the numbers come from stated parameters.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'Does any fit in this course carry a warning from the search?', '["No: every optimiser record the course reads reports `converged` true.", "The damped fit on EKENE-P4 does, since its phi stopped on its bound 0.8.", "Every ses fit whose alpha lands on its upper bound of 1 carries one.", "Yes, the flat SSE surface of the exact line, whose parameters are not identified."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A warning is added only when the search stops at 200000 evaluations before its step rule is met, and no fit in the course reaches that cap. A parameter on its bound is a fitted value like any other, reported in `atBounds` with no warning, and the exact line''s fit converged after 0 moves.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'A fitted alpha lands on 0, its lower bound. What does simple smoothing become there?', '["The naive forecast, the last month carried forward.", "A refusal, since alpha must lie above 0.", "Holt''s linear trend with the trend switched on.", "A level that never moves from its start."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At alpha 0 the new level is all old forecast and none of the new rate, so the level never moves from its start; on EKENE-P1 that is 1176.100000 for ever. The naive forecast is alpha 1. 0 is inside the accepted range, and alpha adds no trend.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'In NIST''s double smoothing example, `atBounds` lists beta = 1. What does a trend weight on that bound mean?', '["A trend frozen for good at its start of 0.8.", "Damping at the strongest rate the box will allow.", "The trend is always the newest change in level.", "The level follows every rate, as the naive forecast does."]'::jsonb, 2, 'beta 1 puts all the trend''s weight on the newest change in level, l_t - l_{t-1}, and none on the old trend. A trend that never changes is beta 0. Damping is phi''s role, and following every rate is alpha on 1.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'The handbook prints a fitted alpha of 0.977 for case nist-6434-ses-fit; the engine returns 0.977276. How should the two be read?', '["A gap in the fourth decimal that points to a different start.", "As a match to 3 decimals, the precision of the printed figure.", "A failure, since the engine should print exactly 0.977.", "As agreement only once alpha is given as 0.977 and held."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A published check compares to the printed precision and never beyond it: 0.977276 rounds to 0.977 at the three decimals the handbook prints. Printing more decimals is no disagreement, so nothing points to a different start. Giving alpha would test a different case from the fitted one.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'Which method from module two does the case nist-6434-ses-alpha-1 test?', '["The naive forecast: simple smoothing at alpha 1.", "Damped with phi 1, which equals Holt.", "Simple smoothing with alpha fitted by the search.", "Holt''s linear trend with its trend weight at 1."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Case nist-6434-ses-alpha-1 is simple smoothing at alpha 1, the naive forecast, on the handbook''s second series; its MSE prints as 8.8867 in the handbook and 8.886667 from the engine. A fitted alpha is the case nist-6434-ses-fit, and the trend weight of 1 belongs to the double smoothing example.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'How many of the handbook''s published figures does the engine match, and at what precision?', '["The 9 figures in the course''s table, each to six decimals.", "All 64, each to six decimals, the engine''s own precision.", "6, one for each golden case, at two decimals throughout.", "All 64, across 6 golden cases, each at the handbook''s printed decimals."]'::jsonb, 3, '6 golden cases carry 64 published figures, and the engine rounds to every one of them at the handbook''s printed decimals, which vary from case to case. The course''s table is a selection, and six decimals is the engine''s printing precision, which no published figure claims.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'A forecast write-up lists `scoredFrom` beside the MSE. What does that item let a reader check?', '["The month the forecast starts from, one step past the series.", "Whether the fit converged before its evaluation cap was reached.", "The MSE''s divisor, the number of scored errors, without refitting.", "Which parameters were given, since each given one shifts where the recursion starts."]'::jsonb, 2, '`scoredFrom` is the index of the first scored residual; it says how many months the start used and so how many errors the MSE divides by. The forecast starts one step past the last month whatever `scoredFrom` is, convergence is read from `converged`, and given parameters are listed in `fixed`.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'Why does the engine start its recursions at the first observation?', '["It is the maximum likelihood start, the one that statistics packages estimate.", "A learner can start the recursion by hand and reproduce every fitted value.", "Starting later would leave the first months of the series without any rate at all.", "The handbook requires it, and the engine must copy the handbook."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course gives the reason: with l_1 = y_1, and b_1 = y_2 - y_1 for the trend methods, anyone can run the recursion by hand from the series and reproduce the engine''s figures. Other tools estimate the starting states. The months are there whatever the start, and the handbook is a check that shares the start; it sets no rule for the engine.'),
  ('forecastml', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'EKENE-P4''s three fitted methods score MSEs of 4585.145790, 6048.241985 and 4502.618003. Which one does a write-up quote, and how?', '["holt''s 6048.241985, since the highest MSE is the most cautious figure to report.", "ses''s 4585.145790 alone, since the flat forecast has no bound to explain.", "The mean of all three MSEs, which spreads the choice of method fairly.", "damped''s 4502.618003, with scoredFrom 2, fitted months 0 to 47, marked in-sample."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The workflow compares by MSE and reports the method it chose with its parameters, `scoredFrom` and the MSE, the months fitted, h, and that the numbers are in-sample. The damped fit has the lowest MSE, 4502.618003, scored from index 2. No rule picks the highest MSE, and averaging MSEs across methods describes no fitted method.');
